{
  "captions" : [
    "A pilot wears small jet engines on each forearm and on the back, all nozzles pointing down. This is the Daedalus layout; the newer suit differs.",
    "Each jet pushes its engine up, and gravity pulls the pilot, suit and fuel down. In a hover the upward parts of the thrusts balance the weight, and side forces and turning moments balance.",
    "The table gives claimed figures for the current suit, which is not the layout shown here.",
    "To steer, the pilot moves one arm, and the forearm engines fixed to it tilt their thrust. The forces no longer balance, so the body moves that way.",
    "From behind: the back engine pushes near the body's centre line. How the load is shared between arm and back engines was not found in this search.",
    "Cut open one arm engine: air enters the intake, and the spinning compressor wheel flings it outward into the diffuser, which squeezes it.",
    "Fuel burns with the oxygen in the squeezed air. The hot gas spins the turbine at 1 turn per input turn, the same as the compressor, because one shaft joins them.",
    "The hot gas speeds up through the nozzle. Thrust is the reaction to that gas being pushed out fast.",
    "Limits: only the shaft turning is solved. Airflow, burning fuel and thrust are not simulated, so the force arrows carry no value."
  ],
